└── Modbus TCP client for reading holding registers from a power meter
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, result.isError, result.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns a response object with .isError() and .registers; minimalmodbus returns raw register lists directly and raises exceptions
    │   on failure instead of an isError() check; pyModbusTCP's read_holding_registers typically returns None on failure rather than an object with isError(), 
    │   requiring different error-handling logic; minimalmodbus is fundamentally serial/RTU-oriented; TCP support is not native and requires additional 
    │   wrapping (e.g. via a TCP-to-serial gateway), which changes client construction significantly; Client constructor signatures differ significantly 
    │   (host/port vs serial port/slave address), requiring changes beyond simple import swaps; unit/slave parameter naming and semantics differ across 
    │   libraries (unit= vs slaveaddress=)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus TCP/serial client functionality that could theoretically read holding registers for this meter. pymodbus's 
        ModbusTcpClient is used here to connect and read holding registers, returning a response object with .isError() and .registers. pyModbusTCP's 
        ModbusClient offers a similar TCP-focused read_holding_registers call. minimalmodbus is primarily serial-oriented (RTU/ASCII) but can be used with some
        TCP wrappers; its read functions return raw lists and raise exceptions instead of returning an object with .isError(), as explicitly noted in the 
        file's own docstring calling out this exact substitution target.

