    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, isError, registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RTU) communication; TCP support is limited/less idiomatic compared to pymodbus; 
    │   minimalmodbus's read functions return raw register lists directly and raise exceptions on error, rather than returning a response object with 
    │   .isError()/.registers; pyModbusTCP's ModbusClient.read_holding_registers returns a plain list (or None on failure) instead of a response object, 
    │   requiring error-handling logic to be rewritten; modbus-tk uses a different connection/master abstraction (modbus_tcp.TcpMaster) and a separate 
    │   execute() call pattern, differing significantly from pymodbus's client.read_holding_registers call signature; unit/slave ID parameter naming and 
    │   defaults differ across libraries (e.g., 'unit' vs 'slaveaddress' vs 'slave')
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── pymodbus is used here strictly as a Modbus TCP client: instantiate ModbusTcpClient(host, port), connect(), then read_holding_registers(address, count, 
        unit=...) returning a response object with .isError() and .registers. minimalmodbus, pyModbusTCP, and modbus-tk all provide Modbus TCP/RTU client 
        functionality that could serve the same role of reading holding registers from a meter, making them functionally interchangeable at the architectural 
        level even though their concrete APIs differ.

