└── finite-state machine engine (state/transition management)
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, fysom, automat
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine, add_transition, model=self dynamic method injection, self.state attribute
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions binds dynamically-generated trigger methods (start_fill, trip, etc.) directly onto the model instance; automat and 
    │   python-statemachine require the state machine logic to live on/around a dedicated class rather than being mixed into an existing model object.; 
    │   Conditions/after callbacks in transitions are resolved by string name lookup on the model; other libraries use direct callable references or 
    │   decorators, requiring restructuring of can_fill/open_inlet/etc.; Wildcard transitions ('*' -> FAULT) are a transitions-specific convenience; equivalent
    │   'from any state' semantics must be manually reproduced in fysom/automat/python-statemachine.; self.state is a transitions convention; other libraries 
    │   expose current state via different attributes/properties (e.g. automat's internal state, python-statemachine's .current_state).; Full migration 
    │   requires rewriting the TankController class structure and guard/callback wiring, not just swapping the import line.
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses `transitions.Machine` to declaratively define states/transitions and dynamically injects trigger methods (start_fill, level_reached, 
        trip, etc.) onto the controller instance, with guard/after callbacks resolved by name on the model. `fysom` offers a similarly event-driven, 
        add_transition-like API and could serve the same functional role with moderate rework. `python-statemachine` and `automat` also implement 
        finite-state-machine semantics but use fundamentally different declarative styles (class-attribute States/Transitions for python-statemachine; 
        decorator-based MethodicalMachine for automat) rather than a Machine object bound to an arbitrary model instance.

