└── Finite-state-machine engine for modeling state transitions
    ├── Candidates : transitions, pysm, fysom, python-statemachine
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine, add_transition, model=self injection, state (self.state) attribute, trigger methods (start_fill, level_reached, start_drain, 
    │   empty, trip)
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions dynamically attaches trigger methods (e.g. self.start_fill()) and a `state` attribute to the model object; 
    │   pysm/fysom/python-statemachine do not support this same dynamic model-injection pattern and require calling transitions on the machine object itself or
    │   restructuring the class hierarchy; conditions/after callback strings (conditions='can_fill', after='open_inlet') resolved by name on the model are 
    │   unique to transitions; other libraries require passing callables or use decorator-based binding; wildcard source transitions ('*' -> FAULT) are 
    │   supported by transitions but need explicit enumeration or different syntax in fysom/pysm; python-statemachine and automat require declaring 
    │   states/transitions as class-level descriptors/decorators, which conflicts with this file's fully dynamic, runtime __init__-based construction; 
    │   migrating would require rewriting guard/action wiring and possibly the class definition itself, not just import lines
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses `transitions.Machine` to dynamically bind states/transitions onto the TankController instance, injecting trigger methods (start_fill, 
        trip, etc.) and a `state` attribute directly onto the model. Other FSM libraries (pysm, fysom, python-statemachine, automat) provide the same 
        conceptual capability -- declarative state/transition definitions with guard conditions and enter/exit callbacks -- and could in principle replace 
        transitions for this role. However their APIs differ substantially: pysm and fysom use event/callback dictionaries or explicit State/Event objects 
        rather than dynamic model injection; python-statemachine and automat require class-based declarative definitions (State() descriptors or 
        MethodicalMachine decorators) rather than instance-level Machine(model=self,...) wiring. The docstring itself flags this as a 'Category C' substitution
        requiring structurally different re-encoding of transitions/guards, confirming this is not a drop-in swap.

