    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions attaches trigger methods dynamically to the passed `model`; python-statemachine instead requires the class itself to inherit 
    │   from StateMachine and declare States/Transitions as class attributes — a structural rewrite, not just an import swap; automat uses a decorator-based 
    │   MethodicalMachine builder pattern (@machine.state, @machine.input, @machine.output) that bears little syntactic resemblance to transitions' 
    │   add_transition calls; fysom has a similar imperative style to transitions but uses a single config dict (`events`) instead of repeated add_transition 
    │   calls, and its callback naming convention (onenter<State>, on<Event>) differs; conditions/after callback resolution differs: transitions resolves 
    │   guard/callback names as strings looked up on the model, while other libraries may require passing callables directly; wildcard transitions ('*' source 
    │   state used for `trip`) are a transitions-specific feature; equivalent behavior must be manually enumerated in some alternatives (e.g. fysom, automat)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions is used purely as a generic FSM engine: it binds transition-trigger methods onto the TankController instance and tracks a `state` 
        attribute. fysom, python-statemachine, and automat all provide comparable state-machine abstractions (states, transitions, guard conditions, and either
        dynamic methods or declarative state objects), so they are functionally interchangeable engines for this same role.

