    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is designed for serial (RTU/ASCII) communication; TCP usage requires additional gateway/wrapper, not a drop-in replacement; 
    │   Error handling differs: pymodbus returns response objects with isError(), while minimalmodbus and modbus-tk typically raise exceptions on failure; API 
    │   signatures differ significantly: pymodbus uses read_holding_registers(address, count, unit=...), pyModbusTCP uses read_holding_registers(address, 
    │   count) without unit kwarg (unit_id set on client), modbus-tk uses execute(slave, cst.READ_HOLDING_REGISTERS, address, count); Response object structure
    │   differs: pymodbus returns object with .registers list, pyModbusTCP returns a plain list or None on failure, modbus-tk returns a tuple; Unit/slave ID 
    │   handling differs across libraries (constructor vs per-call parameter); Connection lifecycle methods differ (connect()/close() semantics vary)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages provide Modbus client functionality capable of reading/writing holding registers over TCP (minimalmodbus primarily targets serial/RTU
        but can be adapted with a TCP-to-serial gateway or its less common TCP support). The file's docstring itself flags pymodbus->minimalmodbus as an 
        intended substitution target, confirming this is a genuine architectural concern in this codebase. pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk are purpose-built TCP 
        Modbus clients with functionally equivalent read/write register operations.

