├── Modbus TCP client for industrial I/O (read/write holding registers)
│   ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
│   ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
│   ├── Pitfalls   : pyModbusTCP returns None on failure and a plain list of register values on success instead of an object with .isError()/.registers, so 
│   │   error-handling logic must be rewritten; modbus-tk uses exceptions (ModbusError) rather than a returned isError() flag, requiring try/except 
│   │   restructuring; unit/slave id parameter naming and position differs across libraries (unit= vs slave= vs implicit in master.execute); connection 
│   │   lifecycle differs: pymodbus needs explicit .connect(), pyModbusTCP connects on open() or lazily, modbus-tk requires constructing a TcpMaster/RtuMaster 
│   │   differently
│   ├── Constraints: requires_connector
│   └── pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient is used here purely as a synchronous TCP Modbus master to read/write holding registers with unit-id addressing and an 
│       isError()/registers response object. pyModbusTCP (ModbusClient) and modbus-tk (both offer TCP master implementations with 
│       read_holding_registers/write_single_register style calls) can perform the same TCP Modbus master role, though each has a distinct response model 
│       (pyModbusTCP returns None/list instead of an object with isError(); modbus-tk raises exceptions instead of an isError() flag) requiring a translation 
│       layer.
└── Modbus master library (RTU-oriented, weaker fit as TCP substitute)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is serial-first; using it for TCP Modbus requires extra glue (e.g. a custom socket-based serial-like wrapper) not natively 
    │   supported; Return values are raw numeric values, not response objects with .isError()/.registers, requiring rewrite of error handling; Instrument 
    │   construction model differs entirely (port/baudrate-based rather than host/port TCP client)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── minimalmodbus is explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as a substitution target, but it is designed primarily for serial/RTU communication 
        with a fundamentally different read/write API (read_register/write_register with different signatures, no isError()/registers object) and no 
        first-class TCP socket client, making it a much weaker functional match than pyModbusTCP or modbus-tk.

