  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - minimalmodbus is serial-first (pyserial-based) and has no native TCP/IP transport; ModbusTcpClient(host, port) has no counterpart, so reaching a Modbus 
TCP device requires fabricating a custom socket-based object that pretends to be a serial.Serial instance underneath minimalmodbus's Instrument -- this is a 
structural transport-layer mismatch, not something a thin call-compatibility wrapper can fully paper over.
    - Instrument construction model (port device path + baudrate/parity/stopbits serial settings, plus per-call slave address) is fundamentally different from 
pymodbus's host/port TCP client model; adapting requires reimplementing device addressing/connection semantics rather than just renaming methods.
    - minimalmodbus's read/write calls return raw numeric values instead of response objects with .isError()/.registers; while this part alone is 
wrapper-fixable, it compounds with the transport-layer gap above rather than standing alone.
    - The codebase's dependency-injected client is expected to behave as a persistent TCP-connected object across multiple calls (temperature, pressure, valve 
control) with a shared .connect() lifecycle; minimalmodbus's serial-only, one-instrument-per-slave-address model does not naturally support this without an ad 
hoc protocol-tunneling layer that goes beyond ordinary glue code.

