  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - paho-mqtt's synchronous, callback-based execution model is fundamentally incompatible with amqtt's asyncio-only API; MqttSink.publish() and 
_default_client() are synchronous functions/methods that must become async and be awaited from an event loop — this is a structural execution-model change that
no wrapper can fully hide from callers of MqttSink.publish() (which is currently defined and used as a plain synchronous method returning an int).
    - paho's publish() returns an MQTTMessageInfo object exposing a synchronous .rc attribute, which the code reads via getattr(info, 'rc', 0); amqtt's 
publish() is a coroutine returning None (or raising on failure) with no analogous return code, so the return-value contract of MqttSink.publish() cannot be 
preserved without changing its type/signature to async and altering its success/failure semantics (exceptions vs. return codes) — a wrapper can adapt the 
*representation* but cannot restore synchronous rc semantics from an inherently async, exception-based API.
    - The module's lazy-import/factory pattern (_default_client) assumes a ready-to-use, connected, synchronous client is returned immediately; amqtt requires 
'await client.connect(uri)' inside a coroutine context, so the factory itself cannot return a fully connected client synchronously — requiring restructuring of
the caller's initialization flow (constructor injection, event loop bootstrapping), which goes beyond wrapper glue code.

