  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - MqttSink.publish() and _default_client() are defined and called synchronously throughout the source, but amqtt's connect/publish/disconnect are 
coroutines requiring an asyncio event loop — this is a structural sync-vs-async execution model mismatch, not merely a signature or return-type difference
    - Wrapping every amqtt coroutine call in asyncio.run() to fake synchronicity would create/tear down an event loop per publish call, breaking connection 
persistence/session semantics that the original paho client relied on — a wrapper can approximate but cannot faithfully replicate the original execution model
    - amqtt's publish does not return anything analogous to paho's MQTTMessageInfo with an .rc attribute, so the getattr(info, 'rc', 0) pattern has no natural 
underlying data to draw from and must be synthesized/faked by a wrapper rather than genuinely mapped
    - The overall design (a plain synchronous class used by presumably synchronous calling code) is incompatible with amqtt's asyncio-only API surface; true 
migration would require restructuring the public interface (async def publish, await client.connect, etc.) throughout the calling code, which is beyond what 
call-site wrappers can transparently provide

